# AUTH AND REGISTRY ONLY.
#
# From pnpm 11 this file is no longer the workspace's configuration file. pnpm reads only
# auth and registry settings from `.npmrc`; every other setting must live in
# `pnpm-workspace.yaml`. The four this workspace used to set here — `node-linker`,
# `strict-peer-dependencies`, `dangerously-allow-all-builds` and `force-legacy-deploy` —
# have moved there, comments and all.
#
# The migration trap, measured on pnpm 12.3.4 rather than read from a changelog: pnpm does
# NOT warn about a settings key it no longer reads from this file. `pnpm config list` under
# pnpm 12 with the old `.npmrc` in place returned the registry entries and nothing else —
# `nodeLinker`, `forceLegacyDeploy` and both others were simply absent, with no diagnostic.
# Two of the four happen to have defaults that match what we wanted, so a half-done
# migration would have looked fine until `pnpm deploy` failed in CI.
#
# The opposite direction is loud, and deliberately so: an unrecognised key in
# `pnpm-workspace.yaml` is `ERR_PNPM_UNRECOGNIZED_WORKSPACE_SETTINGS` when `packageManager`
# pins a version the running pnpm satisfies, which it does here. So settings that live in
# the YAML are checked; settings left behind in this file are not. That asymmetry is the
# reason nothing but auth and registry belongs here now.

# Publish target for the workspace scope. The per-component .npmrc files that carried this
# are deleted; without it the target would be inferred from the environment. The scope is
# @b41ex, so neither half can reach the real Netcracker namespace.
@b41ex:registry=https://npm.pkg.github.com/
registry=https://registry.npmjs.org/
